            "inlines": [
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": "No such odd function exists under the printed assumptions; the conditional is vacuously true.",
                "marks": []
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "p133-q72-short-method",
            "type": "paragraph",
            "fontSize": null,
            "align": "left",
            "spaceBefore": 0,
            "spaceAfter": 2,
            "lineHeight": 1.4,
            "indent": 0,
            "inlines": [
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": "The oddness identity and coefficient comparison give ",
                "marks": []
              },
              {
                "type": "math",
                "latex": "ac=0",
                "display": false
              },
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": " and ",
                "marks": []
              },
              {
                "type": "math",
                "latex": "bd=0",
                "display": false
              },
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": ", contradicting the nonzero coefficients.",
                "marks": []
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      "worked": "Suppose $f$ is odd. For all admissible $x$,\n\n$$\\begin{align*}f(-x)&=-f(x)\\\\\\frac{-ax+b}{-cx+d}&=-\\frac{ax+b}{cx+d}\\\\(-ax+b)(cx+d)&=-(ax+b)(-cx+d)\\\\-acx^2+(bc-ad)x+bd&=acx^2+(bc-ad)x-bd\\\\-2acx^2+2bd&=0.\\end{align*}$$\n\nThis polynomial identity holds for infinitely many $x$. Comparing coefficients gives $ac=0$ and $bd=0$, contradicting $a,b,c,d\\ne0$.\n\nThus no such odd function exists under the printed assumptions. The conditional is vacuously true because its oddness hypothesis cannot occur; the two proposed forms do not give cases satisfying those assumptions.\n\nA direct check confirms the contradiction: since $d\\ne0$, $f(0)=\\frac{b}{d}\\ne0$. However, oddness requires $f(0)=-f(0)$, so $f(0)=0$.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
